Scott 1972, §2. A set is Scott-open when it is an upper set and inaccessible by suprema of nonempty directed sets: whenever a nonempty directed set has its supremum in the set, some member of the set already lies in it.

`ScottOpen.univ` says the universe is Scott-open. It is an upper set, and a nonempty set meets it in any one of its elements.

`ScottOpen.inter` says the intersection of two Scott-open sets is Scott-open. The intersection of upper sets is an upper set. If a nonempty directed set has supremum in the intersection, each factor supplies a member lying in that factor. A common upper bound of those two members lies in the directed set, and the upper-set property puts it in both factors.

`ScottOpen.sUnion` says an arbitrary union of Scott-open sets is Scott-open. A union of upper sets is an upper set. If a nonempty directed set has supremum in the union, the supremum lies in some Scott-open member of the family, and that member supplies an element of the directed set already in the union.
